 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the uniform city court act and the judiciary law, in relation\nto Watertown city court judges",
    "basePrintNo": "S8302",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8302-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2104, UCCA; amd §221-i, Judy L",
    "lawSection": "Uniform City Court Act",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8302\n\nSPONSOR: RITCHIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the uniform city court act and the judiciary law, in\nrelation to Watertown city court judges\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill, introduced at the request of the city of Watertown to reduce\nthe number of Watertown city court judges from two full time to one\nfull-time and one part-time judge.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSections 1 and 2. Section 2104 of the uniform city court act is amended\nto denote that the city of Watertown shall have one full-time and one\npart-time city court judge.\n\nSection 3. Section 221-I of the judiciary law is amended to adjust the\nsalary for the part-time city court judge.\n\nSections 4 and 5 set forth the transition from two full time to one full\nand one part time city court judge and their commensurate salary, as\nwell as any increases in compensation provided by law.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2014 the uniform city court act and the judiciary law were amended to\nincrease the number of city court judges throughout the state, including\nan increase from one full-time and one part-time judge in the city of\nWatertown to two full-time positions.\n\nThe city of Watertown was not consulted at the time of the 2014 changes\nto the law and continues to maintain that the increase to two full-time\ncity court judges is unnecessary and that the caseload of the Watertown\ncity court is adequately handled by one full-time and one-part time\njudge.\n\nFurthermore, the 2014 change in the law calling for two full time city\ncourt judges in the city of Watertown, means that the city must expand\nits court facilities to include an additional court room, at a signif-\nicant cost to the city and its taxpaying citizens. This is occurring at\na time when municipal funds are scarce, budgets are tightening, and cost\ncutting is taking place in other areas, yet the city is being asked to\nshoulder this expense for an increased full time judge that it neither\nrequested nor needs.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone. New Legislation in 2020.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone to the state. The city is expected to save a significant cost which\nwould be required to expand its current court facilities for a second\nfull-time city court judgeship which was never requested by the city and\nis unnecessary.\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the first of April next succeeding the\ndate it shall have become a law.\n",
